import{_ as a,o,c as t,a6 as n}from"./chunks/framework.dL3Vserh.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Operations and joins","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"background/operations-and-joins.md","filePath":"background/operations-and-joins.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"background/operations-and-joins.md"};function r(i,e,p,d,l,c){return o(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="Operations-and-joins" tabindex="-1">Operations and joins <a class="header-anchor" href="#Operations-and-joins" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Operations and joins {#Operations-and-joins}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>GeoDataFrames supplies spatial tables, geometry-column metadata, and I/O. It does not define a second geometry-operation system. Geometry values implement GeoInterface, and <a href="https://juliageo.org/GeometryOps.jl/stable/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GeometryOps.jl</a> provides predicates and geometry operations for those values. Use the <a href="./../how-to/geometry-operations">geometry-operations guide</a> for a table workflow and the GeometryOps documentation for the supported operation set and its assumptions.</p><p>Spatial joins combine that geometry work with tabular join semantics. <a href="https://github.com/JuliaAPlavin/FlexiJoins.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">FlexiJoins.jl</a> composes a GeometryOps predicate into a join condition and returns the matching rows. GeoDataFrames has no GeoPandas-style <code>sjoin</code> method; use FlexiJoins and choose the join shape that fits the rows to retain. The <a href="./../how-to/spatial-joins">spatial-joins guide</a> shows that composition and its candidate filtering.</p><p>This separation keeps table handling, geometric logic, and join policy independent. Before operations or joins that compare coordinates, make both tables use the appropriate CRS; see <a href="./../how-to/reproject-data">reproject data</a>.</p>',4)])])}const u=a(s,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
